akers will highlight advances in predictive modeling, personalized nutrition, and health interventions, and approaches that promote holistic and equitable health outcomes."}]},{"id":"NUTR 510","subject":"NUTR","number":"510","title":"Topics in Nutrition Research","creditHours":"1 TO 3 hours","minHours":1,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"Same as ANSC 525 and FSHN 510. May be repeated in the same term to a maximum of 3 hours and in separate terms to a maximum of 9 hours. Prerequisite: Advanced Biochemistry.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"69141","code":"A","typeCode":"LEC","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"03/23/26-03/26/26","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture","typeCode":"LEC","days":["M","T","W","R"],"start":1020,"end":1190,"location":"292 Animal Sciences Laboratory","instructors":["Cattai de Godoy, M","Oba, P"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"This course explores the gut microbiome and its influence on animals’ health, nutrition, and physiology. Students will learn about microbial diversity across niches, factors shaping gut communities, and the roles of microbiota in host function. Emphasis is placed on current methods for microbiome analysis, including bioinformatic tools and data interpretation. Through discussions and literature review, students will examine emerging research, opportunities, and challenges in studying microbial ecosystems in animals."},{"crn":"69154","code":"C","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"01/20/26-02/23/26","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"108 Bevier Hall","instructors":["Jin, Y","Miller, M"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"In this module, st